{"name":"Outlawed Orange","hex":"#b67350","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"outlawed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["put beyond the limits of the law, forbidden, against the law.","put beyond the limits of the law, declared an outlaw or declared a criminal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of outlaw"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/outlawed"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Outlaw","blocks":[{"text":"An outlaw, in its original and legal meaning, is a person declared as outside the protection of the law. In pre-modern societies, all legal protection was withdrawn from the criminal, so anyone was legally empowered to persecute or kill them. Outlawry was thus one of the harshest penalties in the legal system. In early Germanic law, the death penalty is conspicuously absent, and outlawing is the most extreme punishment, presumably amounting to a death sentence in practice. The concept is known from Roman law, as the status of homo sacer, and persisted throughout the Middle Ages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Newton-knight.jpg/330px-Newton-knight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outlaw","wikidata":"Q254508"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Robin_Hood_Memorial.jpg/500px-Robin_Hood_Memorial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Robin_Hood_Memorial.jpg/500px-Robin_Hood_Memorial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A statue of Robin Hood, a heroic outlaw in English folklore","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobin_Hood_Memorial.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg/500px-Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg/1280px-Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg/500px-Portrait-of-Henry-Danvers-Earl-of-Danby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Henry Danvers, Earl of Danby, was outlawed in 1597 by a coroner's court for the murder of Henry Long. He went to France and joined the French Army; two years later, he was pardoned by Queen Elizabeth I and returned to England.","link":"https://en.wiki